  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nWetting rain is expected areawide Tuesday night and \nWednesday, with a chance of storms. Storms are not expected to \nbe severe, and flooding rain is not anticipated. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nFor the Tuesday night/Wednesday period, we are most likely \nlooking at two primary rounds of activity. The first comes \nmainly later Tuesday night to mid-morning Wednesday as a warm \nfront moves through from SW to NE. We are looking at rain \neverywhere, with perhaps a few embedded storms mainly over \nDowneast. These storms will not be surface-based and will not be\nstrong or severe. We are looking at some pretty decent rain \ntotals from this round of rain, roughly 0.5 to 1 inch, with some\nlocally higher totals Downeast. Advection fog thanks to onshore\nflow could also be an issue Wednesday AM, especially near the \ncoast but possibly inland too.\n\nAfter the first round of rain later Tuesday night to mid-\nmorning Wednesday, there could be a bit of a break from mid-\nmorning to mid-afternoon. However, it's unlikely many places \nwill see any sun, as the moist, muggy onshore flow persists and \neven though there won't be significant rain mid-morning to mid-\nafternoon Wednesday, there could still be intermittent light \nrain and drizzle.\n\nLater Wednesday afternoon comes the second round of activity\nahead of the approaching upper trough. This activity appears to\nbe primarily in the north and more convective in nature, with\nshowers and a few storms. Not a whole lot of surface-based\ninstability to work with except perhaps over the western North\nWoods where there could be some breaks in the clouds. That said,\natmosphere will be very moist and there will be some wind shear.\nIf we do get more surface heating, it could be a different\nstory, but for now think we will just get some showers and a few\nnon-severe storms over the north late Wednesday afternoon.\nAnother 0.25 to 0.5 of rain possible, mainly in the north. The\nrain tapers off Wednesday night, but some fog could linger. A\nmuch drier airmass moves in for Thursday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nRough seas along the Downeast coast Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION... \nUnusually rough seas by late July standards are expected to \npeak on Wednesday. This is usually the quietest time of year on\nthe waters, but seas in the neighborhood of 6-9 feet are \nexpected up to the coastline, coming mainly as a longer 10 \nsecond period swell from the south. What this means is folks \nalong the coast should exercise caution if recreating. \nSituations like this have resulted in people being swept into \nthe ocean by larger waves crashing on the cliffs at Acadia.",
